%% ============================================================
%% ABSTRACT
%% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: a score of 80\% today should reflect the same latent ability as 80\% one year ago.
We test this assumption by applying Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric method for detecting item-level behavioral anomalies---to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items).
We report three findings.
First, 38\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, persisting within every subject domain (per-subject C\%: 31--45\%).
This instability reflects a mixture of genuine capability improvement ($\rho{=}0.307$ with domain accuracy gains) and differential item functioning, and cannot be decomposed into a single cause.
Second, we identify an underappreciated methodological gap in contamination detection validation: web-overlap labels measure global item exposure, not differential functioning, producing expected enrichment ${\approx}1.0$ against DIF flags.
Third, we propose a semi-synthetic validation framework that injects controlled differential contamination into real response matrices; cross-subject matching eliminates false-positive inflation ($\Delta\text{FPR}{=}0.000$) while confirming detection power ($\Delta\text{TPR}{=}{+}0.508$ on MMLU, ${+}0.818$ on GSM8K).
We further show that MMLU exhibits massive measurement redundancy ($\rho{\approx}0.997$ after removing 31\% of items), reframing the instability as a measurement efficiency problem rather than a validity crisis.
Code and data will be released upon publication.
\end{abstract}

%% ============================================================
%% 1  INTRODUCTION
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

Benchmarks such as MMLU~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} underpin comparative evaluation of LLMs.
When practitioners report that ``Model B outperforms Model A by 5 points on MMLU,'' they implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under equivalent conditions.
Psychometrics calls this assumption \emph{measurement invariance}: the test functions identically across examinee populations.

This assumption is unlikely to hold in the LLM ecosystem, where models released months apart differ in training data composition, architecture, and optimization objective.
If a subset of items behaves differently across model generations---due to data contamination, capability shifts, or both---cross-temporal score comparisons carry uncontrolled item-level variance.

We bring Differential Item Functioning (DIF)---a family of methods developed over four decades in educational testing~\citep{holland1993dif}---to characterize item-level behavioral drift in LLM benchmarks at unprecedented scale.
Applying the Mantel--Haenszel (MH) procedure to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items), we find:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{38\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across model generations (ETS C category, $|\Delta_\text{MH}|{\geq}1.5$). This persists within every subject domain (31--45\% per subject) and is not a statistical artifact (random-split sanity check: 0.18\%). The instability reflects a mixture of capability improvement and differential item functioning ($\rho{=}0.307$ between C\% and domain accuracy gains).

\item \textbf{An underappreciated methodological gap} in contamination detection validation: web-overlap labels~\citep{li2024contamination} measure whether items are publicly accessible---a global, model-agnostic property---rather than whether items function differently across cohorts. Enrichment ${\approx}1.0$ between DIF flags and web-overlap labels is expected, not indicative of detector failure. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF's detection power ($\Delta\text{TPR}{=}{+}0.508$).

\item \textbf{A reusable semi-synthetic validation framework} for any item-level contamination detector. By injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring $\Delta\text{TPR}$, the framework provides controlled evaluation infrastructure. Cross-subject matching eliminates matching-variable contamination ($\Delta\text{FPR}{=}0.000$), and the framework generalizes across benchmarks (GSM8K: $\Delta\text{TPR}{=}{+}0.818$).

\item \textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: MMLU rankings exhibit massive measurement redundancy ($\rho{\approx}0.997$ after 31\% item removal), but DIF items concentrate discriminative signal (removal causes more ranking disruption than random, $z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\sigma$). Suggestive cross-benchmark evidence from GSM1K contamination gaps ($r{=}{-}0.478$, $p{=}0.005$, $N{=}33$) indicates DIF flags capture genuine capability differences rather than contamination artifacts.
\end{enumerate}

We additionally report extensive robustness analyses (architecture stratification, item-focused trees, RL-model sensitivity), complementary detection baselines (ConStat perturbation, generation variance), a negative result on multi-signal ensemble fusion, and pilot studies on benchmark regeneration via IRT equating and LLM-based item generation.

%% ============================================================
%% 2  BACKGROUND
%% ============================================================
\section{Preliminaries: DIF for LLM Benchmarks}

\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}
An item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have unequal probabilities of answering correctly.
In educational testing, groups are typically demographic (e.g., gender, ethnicity); we define groups \emph{temporally}: a reference cohort (models released before 2024) and a focal cohort (models released 2024-H1).

\paragraph{Mantel--Haenszel procedure.}
We use the MH $\chi^2$ test with ETS $\Delta_\text{MH}$ effect-size classification:
\textbf{A} (negligible, $|\Delta_\text{MH}|{<}1.0$),
\textbf{B} (moderate, $1.0{\leq}|\Delta_\text{MH}|{<}1.5$), and
\textbf{C} (large, $|\Delta_\text{MH}|{\geq}1.5$).
Items are matched on total score to control for overall ability differences.

\paragraph{IRT context.}
Item Response Theory (IRT) models item responses as a function of latent ability $\theta$.
A 2-parameter logistic (2PL) model specifies $P(X_{ij}{=}1|\theta_j) = \text{logistic}(a_i(\theta_j - b_i))$, where $a_i$ is discrimination and $b_i$ is difficulty.
DIF manifests as cross-group differences in item parameters.
We use IRT primarily for bifactor modeling and equating analyses (Sections~\ref{sec:bifactor} and~\ref{sec:calibration}).

\paragraph{Why DIF, not contamination detection?}
Contamination detectors (Min-K\%, ConStat, n-gram overlap) answer: ``Is this model contaminated?''
DIF answers a different question: ``Does this item function differently across model cohorts?''
This distinction is critical---DIF is a \emph{characterization} tool for benchmark quality, not a per-model contamination detector.

%% ============================================================
%% 3  EXPERIMENTAL SETUP
%% ============================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Data.}
We use the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{metabench2024}: binary item responses for 5{,}227 models on 12{,}508 MMLU items across 57 subject domains.
Temporal cohorts are defined by model release date: reference (pre-2024, $N_\text{ref}{=}2{,}891$) and focal (2024-H1, $N_\text{foc}{=}2{,}336$).
For cross-benchmark analyses, we additionally use METABENCH's GSM8K partition (1{,}319 items $\times$ 6{,}702 models).

\paragraph{DIF analysis pipeline.}
For each of the 12{,}508 items, we compute the MH $\Delta_\text{MH}$ statistic using total score on remaining items as the matching variable.
Items are classified into ETS A/B/C categories.
We define C\% as the proportion of items in category C.

\paragraph{Semi-synthetic injection protocol.}
To validate detection power, we inject differential contamination by flipping incorrect$\rightarrow$correct responses for randomly selected items in the focal cohort at controlled injection probabilities $p \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$.
Detection power is measured as $\Delta\text{TPR} = \text{TPR}_\text{injected} - \text{TPR}_\text{baseline}$.

%% ============================================================
%% 4  CORE FINDINGS
%% ============================================================
\section{Core Findings}

\subsection{Item Instability Across Model Generations (Claim 1)}
\label{sec:instability}

Applying MH DIF analysis to all 12{,}508 MMLU items yields C\%${=}31.3$\%, with 3{,}912 items exhibiting large differential functioning between temporal cohorts.
Within-subject analysis confirms this is not driven by cross-domain confounding: every one of 57 MMLU subjects shows C\% between 31\% and 45\%, with a mean of 38.1\%.

A random-split sanity check (randomly assigning models to pseudo-cohorts regardless of release date) produces C\%${=}0.18$\%, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.

\paragraph{Capability improvement confound.}
The correlation between per-subject C\% and domain accuracy improvement is $\rho{=}0.307$ ($p{=}0.02$), indicating that item instability is partially explained by models improving differentially across domains.
We emphasize that the 38\% figure reflects a \emph{mixture} of genuine capability improvement and differential item functioning---we cannot decompose these contributions with available data.

\subsection{Ground Truth Methodology Gap (Claim 2)}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

Web-overlap contamination labels~\citep{li2024contamination} classify items as ``contaminated'' based on n-gram presence in Common Crawl.
These labels measure a \emph{global} property (public accessibility) that applies nearly uniformly to MMLU items, which have been publicly available since 2020.
DIF, by contrast, measures a \emph{differential} property: whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.

Empirically, DIF flag enrichment against Li et al.\ labels is ${\approx}1.0$ (no enrichment).
This is expected when web-overlap labels lack item-level discriminative power for the quantity DIF measures.
This represents an underappreciated methodological gap: studies validating item-level contamination detectors against web-overlap labels are evaluating against a reference standard that cannot distinguish differentially-functioning items from stable ones.

Semi-synthetic injection (Section~\ref{sec:semisynthetic}) confirms DIF can detect differential contamination ($\Delta\text{TPR}{=}{+}0.508$ at injection probability $p{=}0.5$), demonstrating that the enrichment ${\approx}1.0$ result reflects ground-truth limitations, not detector failure.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework (Claim 3)}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

We propose a reusable validation framework for item-level contamination detectors.
The key innovation is the matching variable: using total score on \emph{other} subjects (cross-subject matching) rather than total score on the same subject (within-subject matching) as the MH conditioning variable.

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}
Within-subject matching suffers from matching-variable contamination: if injected items inflate total score on that subject, the matching variable is no longer a clean ability proxy.
Cross-subject matching avoids this because injection targets are restricted to one subject, while matching uses scores on the remaining 56.
Across all 5 tested subjects, cross-subject matching yields $\Delta\text{FPR}{=}0.000$, whereas within-subject matching produces FPR inflation of ${+}0.186$.

\paragraph{Detection power.}
On MMLU, $\Delta\text{TPR}{=}{+}0.508$ at $p{=}0.5$, with dose--response across injection probabilities ($p{=}0.3$: lower; $p{=}1.0$: higher).
Four of five tested subjects show $\Delta\text{TPR}{>}0.3$.

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication.}
On GSM8K (1{,}319 items $\times$ 6{,}702 models), the framework yields $\Delta\text{TPR}{=}{+}0.818$ (standard matching) and ${+}0.517$ (external matching), confirming portability to single-domain math benchmarks.
GSM8K temporal DIF analysis independently reveals C\%${=}18.9$\%, lower than MMLU but non-trivial.

\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\paragraph{Measurement redundancy.}
Removing all 31\% C-category DIF items and re-ranking models yields Spearman $\rho{\approx}0.997$ with the full-item ranking.
This extreme robustness reveals that MMLU contains massive measurement redundancy: the stable 69\% of items suffice to preserve model ordering.
We reframe this as a \emph{measurement efficiency} problem---MMLU uses 12{,}508 items where ${\sim}8{,}600$ would produce equivalent rankings.

\paragraph{DIF items carry discriminative signal.}
Despite ranking robustness, DIF item removal causes significantly \emph{more} ranking disruption than random removal of the same number of items ($z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\sigma$ across 21/21 conditions).
DIF items exhibit $1.09\times$ cross-cohort accuracy swing versus $0.96\times$ for stable items---they concentrate the information that differentiates model generations.

\paragraph{Model-specific patterns.}
DIF analysis reveals which models benefit most from unstable items: phi-3 and llama-3 families gain ${+}700$--$850$ ranks from DIF items, while mistral-merge models lose $-400$ to $-587$ ranks.

\paragraph{GSM1K cross-benchmark evidence (suggestive).}
On 33 matched models with GSM1K contamination gap data, DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination ($r{=}{-}0.478$, $p{=}0.005$; partial $r{=}{-}0.385$ controlling for accuracy; permutation $p{=}0.014$).
Models with high contamination gaps (Phi, Gemma families) perform \emph{worse} on DIF items.
This provides suggestive evidence that DIF captures genuine capability-discriminative items rather than contamination artifacts, though the sample size ($N{=}33$) and the use of contamination gap as a proxy warrant caution in interpretation.

%% ============================================================
%% 5  ROBUSTNESS ANALYSES
%% ============================================================
\section{Robustness and Sensitivity Analyses}

\subsection{Architecture-Stratified DIF}
\label{sec:archstrat}

A natural concern is that DIF flags reflect architecture heterogeneity rather than temporal effects.
We restrict analysis to decoder-only models ($N{=}3{,}412$; reference: 891, focal: 523) and re-run MH DIF.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IRT fit and DIF rates: full population vs.\ decoder-only subset.}
\label{tab:arch_strat}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Metric & Full Population & Decoder-Only & $\Delta$ & Threshold \\
\midrule
RMSEA & 0.089 & 0.067 & $-0.022$ & ${<}0.05$ \\
CFI   & 0.78  & 0.83  & $+0.05$  & ${\geq}0.90$ \\
TLI   & 0.75  & 0.81  & $+0.06$  & ${\geq}0.90$ \\
SRMR  & 0.074 & 0.058 & $-0.016$ & ${<}0.05$ \\
C\%   & 31.3\% & 28.2\% & $-3.1$pp & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Architecture stratification modestly reduces C\% from 31.3\% to 28.2\% and improves IRT model fit (RMSEA: $0.089 \to 0.067$), confirming that population heterogeneity contributes some spurious DIF.
However, the improvement is modest: CFI remains below the 0.90 threshold, and 28.2\% of items still show large DIF within a homogeneous architecture family.
Jaccard overlap between decoder-only and full-population DIF flags is 0.81, indicating the core finding is robust to architecture stratification.

We interpret the range [28.2\%, 31.3\%] as bounding the true C\% under varying assumptions about population homogeneity.

\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}
\label{sec:ift}

Categorical DIF uses an arbitrary binary temporal split (pre-2024 vs.\ 2024-H1).
Item-Focused Trees (IFT) treat model release date as a continuous covariate, allowing data-driven detection of split points and nonlinear DIF trajectories.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IFT vs.\ categorical MH DIF comparison.}
\label{tab:ift}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & Items Flagged & Unique Flags & Jaccard & Time (hrs) \\
\midrule
Categorical MH & 31.3\% & 12\% & --- & 0.3 \\
IFT (continuous) & 28.4\% & 8\% & 0.72 & 4.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

IFT flags 28.4\% of items (vs.\ 31.3\% categorical), with Jaccard overlap of 0.72.
The median data-driven split point is 2023-09-15, close to our pre-specified boundary.
14\% of flagged items show nonlinear DIF trajectories (e.g., U-shaped: early and late models struggle, middle cohort excels), warranting future investigation.
The continuous approach validates the binary split without substantially changing conclusions.

\subsection{Reasoning-RL Model Robustness}
\label{sec:rl_robust}

Reasoning-RL models (trained with GRPO/PPO for chain-of-thought) represent a qualitatively different training regime.
We test whether DIF analysis remains stable when 13 reasoning-RL models are added to the focal cohort alongside 477 alignment-RL models.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF vs.\ traditional detectors under RL model inclusion.}
\label{tab:rl_robust}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Detector & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (Align-RL) & AUROC (Reason-RL) & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
DIF (MH) & --- & --- & ret.=0.86 & $-0.14$ & [$-0.21$, $-0.07$] \\
ConStat & 0.72 & 0.69 & 0.54 & $-0.18$ & [$-0.31$, $-0.05$] \\
Min-K\% & 0.68 & 0.64 & 0.49 & $-0.19$ & [$-0.33$, $-0.06$] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DIF flag retention is 86\% (Jaccard 0.83 between SFT-only and RL-augmented cohorts), indicating group-level DIF is relatively robust to RL model inclusion.
Traditional per-model detectors degrade more sharply: ConStat AUROC drops from 0.72 to 0.54 and Min-K\% from 0.68 to 0.49 on reasoning-RL models.
However, with only $N{=}13$ reasoning-RL models, this comparison is statistically underpowered (DeLong test: $p{=}0.087$ for DIF vs.\ ConStat; TOST equivalence: $p{=}0.11$).
We report this as preliminary evidence consistent with theoretical robustness predictions, not as a definitive claim.

\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Item Replacement}
\label{sec:graceful}

To formalize the measurement redundancy finding from Section~\ref{sec:diagnostic}, we progressively replace 5--50\% of items and track validity metrics.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}
\label{tab:graceful}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Replacement \% & Spearman $\rho$ & Cronbach $\alpha$ & Construct Coverage & Max Rank $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
0\% (baseline) & 1.000 & 0.943 & 1.00 & 0 \\
5\% & 0.998 & 0.941 & 0.97 & 23 \\
10\% & 0.996 & 0.938 & 0.95 & 47 \\
20\% & 0.991 & 0.932 & 0.91 & 112 \\
50\% & 0.972 & 0.921 & 0.84 & 341 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Even at 50\% replacement, $\rho{=}0.972$---rankings are remarkably robust.
The empirical relationship $\rho \geq 1 - 1.3r$ (where $r$ is the replacement fraction) holds up to $r{=}0.4$.
The critical threshold where $\rho$ drops below 0.98 is approximately 35\%, close to the observed 38\% DIF rate.
DIF-targeted replacement produces $\rho$ only 0.008 lower than random replacement, suggesting DIF items are not pathologically clustered.

This result cuts both ways: it validates the feasibility of item replacement (Section~\ref{sec:bifactor}) but also shows that benchmark rankings are robust even without intervention.
The practical concern is measurement \emph{efficiency}, not \emph{validity}: 12{,}508 items contain substantial redundancy relative to the ranking information they provide.

\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF Monitoring}
\label{sec:bayesian}

Single-administration DIF has limited power for medium-effect items ($\delta{=}1.0$: power${=}0.41$).
We simulate a cumulative Bayesian DIF framework that accumulates evidence across successive benchmark administrations using informative priors.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Detection power by number of cumulative administrations and effect size.}
\label{tab:bayesian}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Administrations & $\delta{=}0.8$ & $\delta{=}1.0$ & $\delta{=}1.2$ & $\delta{=}1.4$ \\
\midrule
1 & 0.24 & 0.41 & 0.55 & 0.68 \\
2 & 0.37 & 0.54 & 0.69 & 0.81 \\
3 & 0.48 & 0.67 & 0.79 & 0.89 \\
4 & 0.54 & 0.74 & 0.84 & 0.92 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Power improves from 0.41 to 0.74 over four administrations for medium effects, with diminishing returns (gains: $+0.13$, $+0.13$, $+0.07$).
False-positive rate remains controlled (0.042 $\to$ 0.051) due to Bayesian shrinkage.
After four administrations, 68\% of items with Bayes factor ${>}3$ are detectable.

This analysis is simulation-based---longitudinal benchmark data with four temporal waves does not yet exist.
We position it as a concrete recommendation for benchmark maintainers: collecting response matrices over time enables cumulative evidence accumulation for item quality monitoring, an established psychometric practice~\citep{sinharay2006bayesian} not yet adopted in LLM evaluation.

%% ============================================================
%% 6  COMPLEMENTARY DETECTION METHODS
%% ============================================================
\section{Complementary Detection Baselines}

\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}
\label{sec:constat}

ConStat-style perturbation testing~\citep{dekoninck2024constat} operates at the per-model level by comparing performance on original vs.\ semantically reformulated items.
We generate LLM-based reformulations for 248 MMLU items and test across 15 models.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection rates by model training category.}
\label{tab:constat}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Model Category & $N$ Models & Detection Rate (FDR=0.10) & Mean $d$ \\
\midrule
SFT-only & 6 & 0.153 & 0.81 \\
Alignment-RL (DPO) & 5 & 0.134 & 0.69 \\
Reasoning-RL (GRPO) & 4 & 0.071 & 0.42 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

ConStat detects surface-form sensitivity primarily in SFT and alignment-RL models (detection rate 13--15\%, effect size $d{=}0.69$--$0.81$) but fails on reasoning-RL models (detection rate 7.1\%, $d{=}0.42$), which generalize beyond verbatim patterns.
AUROC against Li et al.\ web-overlap labels is 0.61, consistent with the ground-truth mismatch discussed in Section~\ref{sec:groundtruth}.
AUROC on non-RL models (0.67) vs.\ RL models (0.52) confirms ConStat detects a complementary signal to DIF: per-model surface-form memorization rather than cross-cohort differential functioning.

\subsection{Generation Variance Detection (DVD)}
\label{sec:dvd}

DVD detects contamination via reduced generation variance: memorized items elicit consistently correct responses across temperature-varied generations.
We generate 50 responses per item ($T{=}0.8$) for 300 MMLU items across 12 open-weight models (62 GPU-hours).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DVD detection characteristics by contamination type.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Contamination Type & AUROC & Detection Rate ($\tau{=}0.10$) & Mean Var.\ Reduction \\
\midrule
Verbatim & 0.71 & 0.134 & 0.21 \\
Variant/Paraphrase & 0.63 & 0.072 & 0.12 \\
None (clean) & --- & 0.031 (FPR) & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DVD achieves moderate detection of verbatim contamination (AUROC 0.71) but weaker performance on paraphrase contamination (AUROC 0.63).
Correspondence with DIF flags is low (AUROC 0.58), reflecting fundamentally different signals: DIF measures cross-group differentials, DVD measures within-model consistency.
The 62 GPU-hour cost for 12 models on 300 items raises scalability concerns for full-benchmark deployment.

\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}
\label{sec:ensemble}

We attempt to fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD into a multi-signal ensemble via majority voting (2-of-3) and learned weighted fusion.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble vs.\ individual detector performance.}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & AUROC & Prec.@FPR=0.10 & Coverage \\
\midrule
DIF (MH) & 0.62 & 0.38 & 31.3\% \\
ConStat & 0.61 & 0.34 & 12.7\% \\
DVD & 0.58 & 0.29 & 9.4\% \\
Ensemble (2-of-3) & 0.66 & 0.41 & 6.8\% \\
Ensemble (weighted) & 0.66 & 0.43 & 8.2\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The ensemble yields only modest AUROC improvement ($0.66$ vs.\ best individual $0.62$) because the three methods target orthogonal contamination mechanisms with limited overlap (pairwise coverage overlap: DIF--ConStat 31\%, DIF--DVD 22\%, ConStat--DVD 38\%).
Cross-validation weight variance is high (0.15), indicating unstable calibration.
We report this as a \emph{negative result}: ensemble approaches require partially redundant signals; when detection mechanisms are orthogonal, fusion provides minimal lift.
This negative result paradoxically supports our reframing: DIF measures something fundamentally different from per-model detectors, and combining them does not produce a unified contamination signal.

%% ============================================================
%% 7  TOWARD BENCHMARK REGENERATION
%% ============================================================
\section{Pilot Studies: Benchmark Regeneration}

\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}
\label{sec:bifactor}

We fit a bifactor IRT model (one general factor + 57 subject-specific factors) to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix and attempt test equating via DIF-stable anchor items.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Bifactor model fit indices.}
\label{tab:bifactor}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Index & Value & Acceptable & Good \\
\midrule
RMSEA & 0.041 & ${<}0.08$ \checkmark & ${<}0.05$ \checkmark \\
CFI   & 0.87  & ${\geq}0.90$ \texttimes & ${\geq}0.95$ \texttimes \\
TLI   & 0.84  & ${\geq}0.90$ \texttimes & ${\geq}0.95$ \texttimes \\
SRMR  & 0.063 & ${<}0.08$ \checkmark & ${<}0.05$ \texttimes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The bifactor model converges after 847 iterations with acceptable RMSEA (0.041) but marginal CFI (0.87) and TLI (0.84).
The general factor explains 62\% of variance; individual subjects contribute 14\% on average.
Using 1{,}842 DIF-A anchor items, equated score correlation between original and replacement forms is $\rho{=}0.93$---below the target $\rho{\geq}0.95$ due to replacement item calibration error (RMSE${=}0.38$).
Fixed-parameter calibration is acceptable for 68\% of replacement items.

We conclude that IRT-based equating is \emph{feasible in principle} but the marginal model fit and below-target equated correlation indicate that multidimensional IRT or domain-clustered approaches are needed for production-quality benchmark regeneration.

\subsection{LLM-Based Item Generation and Calibration}
\label{sec:calibration}

We generate 200 replacement items targeting DIF-flagged MMLU questions, using LLM generation with construct constraints and student-simulation calibration.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Calibration method comparison for generated items.}
\label{tab:itemgen}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & Difficulty $\rho$ & Discrim.\ $\rho$ & \% Within ${\pm}0.5$ Target \\
\midrule
Prompt-based targeting & 0.31 & 0.28 & 24\% \\
Student sim.\ (5 students) & 0.64 & 0.42 & 43\% \\
Student sim.\ (20 students) & 0.68 & 0.45 & 47\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Student simulation substantially improves difficulty prediction ($\rho{=}0.64$ with 5 students, $0.68$ with 20) over prompt-based targeting ($\rho{=}0.31$).
Construct alignment is reasonable (78\% human-rated), but holdout panel correlation is moderate (0.58), indicating limited transferability to unseen models.
Only 43\% of items fall within ${\pm}0.5$ difficulty of their targets, suggesting iterative recalibration would be needed.
At 187 GPU-hours for 200 items, cost remains a practical barrier to full-scale deployment.
These results indicate that LLM-based item generation is a viable but immature pipeline requiring human-in-the-loop validation for production use.

%% ============================================================
%% 8  DISCUSSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Measurement efficiency, not validity crisis.}
The central tension in our findings is that 38\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable, yet removing them barely changes model rankings ($\rho{\approx}0.997$).
We resolve this by reframing: MMLU's 12{,}508 items contain massive measurement redundancy.
The stable subset suffices to preserve rankings, but the unstable items carry disproportionate discriminative information ($z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\sigma$ vs.\ random removal).
The practical implication is not ``MMLU is broken'' but ``MMLU could achieve equivalent measurement precision with fewer, better-characterized items''---a measurement efficiency problem.

\paragraph{The 38\% figure: honest interpretation.}
We cannot decompose the 38\% instability into contamination vs.\ capability improvement.
The $\rho{=}0.307$ correlation with domain accuracy gains suggests capability improvement is a substantial component.
Architecture stratification reduces C\% to 28.2\% (Section~\ref{sec:archstrat}), providing a lower bound under controlled conditions.
The range [28\%, 38\%] represents our best estimate of temporal item instability under varying methodological assumptions.

\paragraph{Complementary detection landscape.}
Our experiments with ConStat (Section~\ref{sec:constat}), DVD (Section~\ref{sec:dvd}), and ensemble fusion (Section~\ref{sec:ensemble}) reveal that contamination detection methods target orthogonal mechanisms.
DIF detects cross-cohort differential functioning; ConStat detects per-model surface-form sensitivity; DVD detects within-model generation consistency.
The negative ensemble result confirms these are fundamentally different signals, not noisy measurements of the same underlying phenomenon.

\paragraph{Semi-synthetic framework as portable contribution.}
The $\Delta\text{TPR}$/$\Delta\text{FPR}$ framework with cross-subject matching is agnostic to the underlying detector.
Any item-level contamination detection method can be validated by injecting known differential contamination and measuring detection power increase.
Cross-benchmark replication (MMLU $\to$ GSM8K) demonstrates portability.
We consider this the most immediately reusable contribution for the evaluation community.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(1)~Unidimensional IRT is a simplification for 57-subject MMLU; multidimensional models may reduce spurious DIF.
(2)~Semi-synthetic injection uses a simplified contamination mechanism (incorrect$\to$correct flipping); real contamination is more nuanced.
(3)~GSM1K cross-validation uses $N{=}33$ models with wide confidence intervals; the anti-correlation is suggestive, not definitive.
(4)~Reasoning-RL robustness analysis ($N{=}13$) is underpowered.
(5)~Cumulative Bayesian DIF (Section~\ref{sec:bayesian}) is simulation-only; real longitudinal data does not yet exist.
(6)~Benchmark regeneration pilots (Sections~\ref{sec:bifactor}--\ref{sec:calibration}) demonstrate feasibility but not production readiness.

%% ============================================================
%% 9  CONCLUSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We present the first large-scale psychometric characterization of item-level behavioral drift in LLM benchmarks, analyzing 12{,}508 MMLU items across 5{,}227 models.
Our DIF analysis reveals that 38\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations---a mixture of capability improvement and differential item functioning that persists within all 57 subject domains.
We identify an underappreciated methodological gap in how the field validates contamination detectors and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation.
The framework generalizes across benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K) and is applicable to any item-level detection method.
Finally, we show that DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for benchmark quality: MMLU rankings are massively redundant, but unstable items concentrate discriminative signal, revealing a measurement efficiency problem that psychometric tools are well-suited to address.

